      },
      "worked": "Let $D$ be the stated common domain. Both inverses are strictly increasing: for either function, a larger output must come from a larger input because the original function is strictly increasing.\nFor $x\\in f(D)\\cap g(D)$, set $u=f^{-1}(x)$ and $v=g^{-1}(x)$. Then $f(u)=x$ and $g(v)=x$.\nSince $f(u)>g(u)$, we have $g(u)<g(v)$. As $g$ is strictly increasing, $u<v$. Therefore\n$$f^{-1}(x)<g^{-1}(x)\\qquad\\text{for }x\\in f(D)\\cap g(D).$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
